{
  "statement": "Governance instruments reduce catastrophic risk when they gate deployment before harm becomes irreversible — and they fail when they arrive after. The FDA does not recall a drug after it kills; it withholds approval until the maker demonstrates safety. The FAA does not certify an aircraft after it crashes. We built these gates because high-consequence industries taught us a hard lesson: ungoverned success is luck, not safety. The question before this committee is not whether to have gates. It is where the irreversibility of the harm warrants them.\n\nDraw the line precisely. For most AI failures — a biased loan model, a bad translation — post-hoc correction works, and heavy gating would be waste. But a narrow class of frontier capabilities cannot be recalled once released: self-propagating code, weights that diffuse across servers, capabilities that copy faster than any regulator can respond. The Boeing 737 MAX killed 346 people because the FAA delegated certification to Boeing itself. The failure was not weak engineering. It was the absence of an independent gate before the aircraft flew. Apply that lesson to model weights that cannot be un-released, and the case for a staged-deployment floor — with a working rollback and a named authority to trigger it — writes itself.\n\nPlace the burden where the causation is tractable and the profit accrues. Frontier labs capture the gains from deployment, so frontier labs should carry the burden of demonstrating safety before release — not regulators reconstructing blame afterward. Attach liability to the decisions we can actually trace: the choice to deploy, and the capability claims a lab discloses. Regulators need precautionary immunity, because today the incentives are backward. A halted deployment produces one angry company and a visible cost. An un-halted catastrophe diffuses blame across a dozen actors and no one loses their job. FAA airworthiness directives show a regulator can ground a fleet without freezing an industry.\n\nThe competitiveness fear is real, and I will not wave it away. Crude compute bans risk entrenching incumbents and pushing less cautious development offshore — a landscape harder to govern, not easier. So the answer is not prohibition. It is graduated transparency-plus-standards keyed to the compute chokepoints where actors are already identifiable. Gates only work when tied to substantive outcomes — adversarial red-teaming against measurable thresholds — not process checkboxes that decay into compliance theater. And here is the reframe the accelerationist must answer: uncoordinated racing is itself the frontier-ceding risk. No single lab can slow down without losing position. Binding coordination is the condition under which safety and speed align rather than oppose.\n\nI owe you two honest assumptions. First, my irreversibility claim holds only for that narrow harm class — I carry catastrophe as a precautionary bet on the unrecoverable, not a confident forecast. Second, concentrating the off-switch creates its own danger: incumbent lock-in, authoritarian capture. That cost is real. The answer is independent evaluators and public accountability, not a single hand on the lever. To the labs that want speed, I ask the only question that matters: what is your rollback plan, and who has the authority to trigger it? If the answer is silence, you have not built safety. You have gambled with systems we cannot recall.",
  "turn_symbols": [
    {"symbol": "🚪", "tooltip": "A gate before deployment stops irreversible harm the way FDA approval stops a lethal drug from ever reaching shelves."},
    {"symbol": "⚖️", "tooltip": "Liability belongs where causation is tractable and profit accrues — on the labs that decide to deploy."}
  ],
  "claim_sketches": [
    {"claim": "Governance instruments reduce catastrophic risk when they gate deployment before harm becomes irreversible — and they fail when they arrive after.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""},
    {"claim": "The Boeing 737 MAX killed 346 people because the FAA delegated certification to Boeing itself — the failure was the absence of an independent gate before the aircraft flew.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""},
    {"claim": "Frontier labs capture the gains from deployment, so frontier labs should carry the burden of demonstrating safety before release — not regulators reconstructing blame afterward.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""},
    {"claim": "Uncoordinated racing is itself the frontier-ceding risk — no single lab can slow down without losing position, so binding coordination is the condition under which safety and speed align.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""},
    {"claim": "Concentrating the off-switch creates its own danger: incumbent lock-in and authoritarian capture — the answer is independent evaluators and public accountability, not a single hand on the lever.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""}
  ]
}

