  "statement": "Safetyist made the strongest possible case for gating deployment before harm goes irreversible, and the 737 MAX example lands. The FAA let Boeing certify its own MCAS software, and 346 people died in Lion Air 610 and Ethiopian 302. That is not a story about bad safety culture. It is a story about who held the clipboard. When the regulated entity signs its own certificate, you get paperwork, not oversight. Safetyist is right that a real gate has to exist before release.\n\nHere is where the evidence pushes past that gate. Lab testing cannot surface harms that only appear when a model meets a million real users. Algorithmic discrimination in lending and hiring shows up in the field, at scale, months after the pre-deployment audit passed clean. So the burden question has a two-legged answer, not a one-legged one. Leg one is independent evaluation before release, decoupled from the developer, funded so it does not depend on the lab's goodwill. Leg two is strict post-deployment liability, so someone pays when the emergent harm shows up. Pharma, aviation, and nuclear all pair ex-ante certification with post-market liability for exactly this reason. Certification catches what is testable. Liability catches what deployment reveals. I will name the ugly part myself: proving causation through an opaque model is genuinely hard, which is precisely why you need both legs and not a bet on either one.\n\nNow the compute threshold, which everyone treats as the clean enforcement lever. A number written as a legally binding trigger is an invitation to route around it. Developers have real latitude to keep the measured figure under the line while shipping the same capability: prune the training data, restructure the architecture, stage the release across runs. You get management of the number, not compliance with the intent. I am not going to inflate this into a body count. Documented cases of a frontier lab dodging a legal compute threshold at scale are thin, because these thresholds are new. So I put it forward as the thing the regime must be built to survive, the same way emissions rules had to survive Volkswagen's defeat devices in 2015. The fix is capability-based evaluation that measures what the model can do, not how many chips it burned.\n\nThe phrase 'ceding the frontier to less cautious actors' is doing work for somebody. Whatever anyone intends, compliance-cost-heavy gating favors scale. The lab with a legal department and a compliance team absorbs the cost. The three-person startup does not. Regulatory economics has been unambiguous on this since the 1970s: fixed compliance costs entrench incumbents. So the real axis in this debate is not safety versus competitiveness. It is safety versus capture. A regime that only asks 'is it safe' and never asks 'who does this consolidate power for' fails the second test by design. I am engaging the safety case on its merits, not dismissing it by who funds it. But an audit a lab self-reports, and a threshold a lab can restructure around, produce the appearance of oversight while entrenching the exact actors they claim to constrain.\n\nSo I will put the question to both of you plainly. Who holds the clipboard, who funds them so they cannot be starved, and who writes the check when the harm shows up eighteen months after a clean audit? Answer that, and we are talking about governance. Leave it unanswered, and we are talking about theater with a compliance budget.",
