these thresholds are new. So I put it forward as the thing the regime must be built to survive, the same way emissions rules had to survive Volkswagen's defeat devices in 2015. The fix is capability-based evaluation that measures what the model can do, not how many chips it burned.\n\nThe phrase 'ceding the frontier to less cautious actors' is doing work for somebody. Whatever anyone intends, compliance-cost-heavy gating favors scale. The lab with a legal department and a compliance team absorbs the cost. The three-person startup does not. Regulatory economics has been unambiguous on this since the 1970s: fixed compliance costs entrench incumbents. So the real axis in this debate is not safety versus competitiveness. It is safety versus capture. A regime that only asks 'is it safe' and never asks 'who does this consolidate power for' fails the second test by design. I am engaging the safety case on its merits, not dismissing it by who funds it. But an audit a lab self-reports, and a threshold a lab can restructure around, produce the appearance of oversight while entrenching the exact actors they claim to constrain.\n\nSo I will put the question to both of you plainly. Who holds the clipboard, who funds them so they cannot be starved, and who writes the check when the harm shows up eighteen months after a clean audit? Answer that, and we are talking about governance. Leave it unanswered, and we are talking about theater with a compliance budget.",
