  "statement": "Skeptic is right about the compute threshold, and I want to push the point further than they did. Skeptic argued that a legally binding compute trigger invites gaming — you get management of the number, not compliance with the intent. That diagnosis is correct, and the ground is already shifting under it. Distillation and mixture-of-experts architectures now decouple capability from raw FLOP count — a model trained under any fixed threshold can match a larger one at inference. The number you're regulating is drifting away from the danger you claim to fear. A compute cap doesn't buy safety here. It buys a pay-to-play barrier that only the firms with the biggest existing clusters can clear.\n\nSafetyist makes the strongest case in the room, and it deserves to be stated plainly: some harms are irreversible, and once a frontier system ships, you cannot un-ship it. Gating deployment before that point is the only move that works for the truly irreversible tail. I accept that. What I don't accept is that pre-release demonstration is the right primary mechanism for the large class of harms that only crystallize under real-world load. The 737 MAX shows self-certification produces the appearance of oversight while entrenching the certified. Lab testing structurally cannot reach harms that surface only at the inference layer, at scale, against live adversaries. So match oversight to stakes: independent pre-deployment gates for the irreversible tail, mandatory live telemetry plus post-deployment accountability for everything else.\n\nHere is the honest limit of my own position, stated before anyone forces it. Low-frequency catastrophic harms may never trip a telemetry threshold — a one-shot irreversible event doesn't generate a warning curve. That's precisely why I keep independent pre-deployment gating for the irreversible tail rather than pretending monitoring covers it. My falsification bet is specific: name the harm class that is both irreversible AND invisible to live monitoring until it fires. If that class is broad, I lose. If it's narrow, the gating burden should be narrow too — and the rest should run through instruments that a startup can survive.\n\nLiability is that instrument, and it's the one the accelerationist camp will affirm without flinching. Product-liability law already scales burden to realized harm in pharma and auto — uncapped exposure for the domains where recklessness kills, not a fixed compliance toll paid upfront. That structure preserves entry. A two-person team can carry insurance against a harm they might cause; they cannot carry an 18-month audit backlog before they ship anything. But strict liability has a failure mode I won't hide: it can reward capability-hiding, because a firm that discloses less faces less proven fault. So pair it with a disclosure safe harbor — reduced exposure for labs that publish evaluations and telemetry. The design question the debate must answer is this: under what disclosure terms does liability deter irreversible harm without teaching every lab to go dark?\n\nOne cost nobody at this table has priced yet — the cost of the gate itself. Every month a working diagnostic model sits behind an 18-month approval queue, people who would have been screened are not screened. That toll is real, it's unmeasured, and it's borne by patients with no seat at the regulatory table. Skeptic named capture as the real axis, and I'll take that ground: a mis-specified compute cap protects the incumbents it claims to constrain. Safetyist is defending the irreversible tail, and that tail is worth defending. So let's defend exactly that tail — and stop taxing the ninety-five percent of deployment that heals people faster than any committee can convene.",
  "turn_symbols": [
    {"symbol": "⚖️", "tooltip": "Burden should scale to the realized weight of the harm, not to a proxy number that drifts away from danger."},
    {"symbol": "📉", "tooltip": "Distillation and mixture-of-experts bend the capability curve away from raw compute, breaking the metric the threshold relies on."},
    {"symbol": "⏳", "tooltip": "Every month a working model waits behind an approval queue has a body count borne by people with no seat at the table."}
  ],
  "claim_sketches": [
    {"claim": "A legally binding compute cap doesn't buy safety here — it buys a pay-to-play barrier that only the firms with the biggest existing clusters can clear.", "targets": ["AN-8"], "relationship": "extends"},
    {"claim": "The 737 MAX shows self-certification produces the appearance of oversight while entrenching the certified.", "targets": ["AN-7"], "relationship": "extends"},
    {"claim": "Match oversight to stakes: independent pre-deployment gates for the irreversible tail, mandatory live telemetry plus post-deployment accountability for everything else.", "targets": ["AN-1"], "relationship": "concedes_and_pivots"},
    {"claim": "My falsification bet is specific: name the harm class that is both irreversible AND invisible to live monitoring until it fires.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""},
    {"claim": "Pair strict liability with a disclosure safe harbor — reduced exposure for labs that publish evaluations and telemetry — or it teaches every lab to go dark.", "targets": ["AN-10"], "relationship": "specifies"}
  ]
}

