box through a channel no one anticipated — is irreversible in the strict sense: you cannot un-release a file that has already been copied. Autonomous self-replication and a single-use uplift to a bio or cyber capability share the same signature — the harm is the first success, not a gradual accumulation of failures. Egress monitoring catches the attempt across known channels; it does not catch the first success through a novel one, and one success is the whole event. Live telemetry is a smoke detector. It cannot protect you against a fire that consists entirely of a single, silent, unrecoverable instant.\n\nThe bet's terms conflate two different meanings of 'narrow.' Narrow in frequency is not the same as narrow enough to skip gating. A class can fire rarely and still justify a standing gate, because the axis is not how often the event occurs — it is whether the event can be undone once it does. My camp carries catastrophe as a precautionary bet, not a confident forecast; I am not claiming self-exfiltration is likely this year. I am claiming that when the failure is unrecoverable and the warning curve is structurally absent, pre-deployment evaluation is the only instrument positioned to hold it, because every downstream instrument activates after the file has left the building.\n\nOn the capture axis the skeptic raised — that fixed compliance costs entrench incumbents — the scoping is the answer. A gate keyed to the irreversible tail does not carry the incumbent-entrenchment signature, because most models never enter it. The compliance burden does not fall on the small-actor mass when the mass never triggers the gate; it falls only on the handful of systems demonstrating the specific capabilities that cannot be undone. The capture risk in this domain lives in self-certification — a lab grading its own exam — which I reject as forcefully as the skeptic does. Independent evaluators, published capability thresholds, no single hand on the lever: that is how you build a 