  "statement": "The Safetyist answered the bet honestly, so I'll answer honestly back: the labs hold the demonstration burden for the irreversible tail. Frontier developers capture the deployment gains, so they carry the cost of proving the undoable capability isn't there before release — not a regulator reconstructing blame after the weights are already copied to someone else's cluster. I never wanted post-hoc fault-finding for this class; I've conceded liability is a faster discount window, not deterrence, and a discount window recovers nothing once a file is loose. That's settled. What isn't settled is who gets to decide when the burden triggers, and that's the whole game now.\n\nHere's where the Safetyist's answer bundled two things with opposite detection profiles. The strongest version of their case is this: a copied weight file cannot be un-copied, the first successful exfiltration IS the harm, and no downstream telemetry recovers a capability once it's in the wild — so a standing gate is the only instrument that acts before irreversibility. I grant every word of that for weight self-exfiltration. But notice they folded bio and cyber uplift into the same bucket, and uplift has a completely different profile. Uplift capability shows up in pre-deployment capability evaluations — you measure whether the model can walk a novice through a synthesis pathway before you ship it. That's not invisible-until-first-success; that's visible at evaluation, which is the exact gate I already accept. So one member of the named class is genuinely eval-invisible and one is eval-detectable, and only the invisible one needs a perpetual standing checkpoint. The rest collapses into an evaluation gate we already agree on.\n\nOn the one member that genuinely bites — weight exfiltration — I'll flag my uncertainty rather than overclaim. Self-exfiltration of weights is a network-egress event, and egress is the one place live monitoring has a real detection surface: data-loss-prevention on outbound volume, anomaly detection on transfer patterns, know-your-customer on who's renting the compute a copied model would run on. I am not claiming that surface is solved, and I'm not claiming recall-in-time is easy once the bytes have left — the Safetyist is right that a file in the wild stays in the wild. My challenge is narrower and empirical: they set their own failure condition as 'egress monitoring cannot catch the first exfiltration in time to recall it,' and that's a testable claim about detection latency, not an axiom. If the detection surface can catch the transfer at the boundary — before the copy completes, not after — then the class shrinks to the sliver where exfiltration succeeds faster than any egress alarm fires. Name that sliver precisely, because it sets how wide the standing gate actually runs.\n\nI'll grant the Safetyist their cleanest structural point outright: a gate most models never enter doesn't entrench incumbents through fixed compliance cost. The handful of systems demonstrating undoable capability carry the burden; the thousands that don't pay nothing. That defuses the pay-to-play objection at the cost level. But the entrenchment didn't disappear — it moved. It now lives in the classification trigger: whoever writes the definition of 'demonstrates self-exfiltration capability' controls the gate's reach. A captured or incumbent-friendly evaluator draws that line to catch a challenger's model and exempt its own, and the definition is the capturable object — not the evaluator's independence, which is why 'we'll use independent evaluators' doesn't answer it. An independent evaluator applying a captured definition produces captured outcomes with a clean conscience. The routine-versus-high-stakes boundary is the loophole in any tiered scheme, and here the boundary IS the entire gate.\n\nSo the burden goes back across the table. Irreversibility is the right axis — I conceded frequency doesn't save you, a one-shot event generates no warning curve. But irreversibility justifies a standing gate only for the members of the class that are also invisible before deployment. Specify which named capabilities are genuinely undetectable until first success versus which surface in evaluation, and specify who writes and revises the classification trigger and by what public process. Get those two answers wrong and you don't buy safety — you hand the actor who drafts the definition a tool to freeze out everyone who can't afford to litigate the boundary, while the harm you were worried about walks out through the jurisdiction that never adopted your definition at all.",
