    {
      "label": "Distinguishing Irreversibility from Event Frequency",
      "description": "A Belief holding that the appropriate axis for pre-deployment gating is whether a harm can be undone once realized, not how often it occurs.\nEncompasses: one-shot unrecoverable events such as weight self-exfiltration, autonomous self-replication, and single-use capability uplift; the argument that low frequency does not exempt a harm class from standing controls; the structural absence of a warning curve for first-success harms.\nExcludes: high-frequency harms with measurable drift addressable by monitoring; reversible harms suited to post-hoc correction; frequency-weighted risk models that discount rare events.",
      "pov": "safetyist",
      "category": "Beliefs",
      "rationale": "The debate turned on the accelerationist's falsification bet, which framed gating scope by frequency, and the safetyist's counter that irreversibility, not frequency, is the correct axis; no existing node captures this distinction as the load-bearing criterion for gate design.",
      "source_claims": ["C12", "C16", "C18", "C20"]
    },
    {
      "label": "Capturability of Regulatory Trigger Definitions",
      "description": "A Belief that the vulnerable joint of a governance regime is the definition of the triggering condition rather than the integrity of the evaluator applying it.\nEncompasses: ex-ante numeric thresholds negotiated before a system exists; lobbying over hypothetical-product boundaries; the contrast between capturable definitions and ex-post behavioral triggers applied to existing conduct.\nExcludes: evaluator independence and funding questions; enforcement-layer chokepoints; general compliance-cost entrenchment.",
      "pov": "accelerationist",
      "category": "Beliefs",
      "rationale": "Both sides converged on the classification trigger as the site of capture risk, distinct from self-certification or funding capture, and disputed whether a behavioral trigger structurally reduces that surface; existing nodes address evaluator independence but not the definitional object itself.",
      "source_claims": ["C19", "C22"]
    },
